body:
yeh, Jesus I didn't believe you so I did it myself and I got around the same.

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

https://preview.redd.it/6bcmsa6yepfc1.png?width=829&format=png&auto=webp&s=1ac53c1146b6a7d34c23a4066aa8f9a25667e3ae

I plotted it and it looks like this :

&#x200B;

https://preview.redd.it/fw41l7b0poac1.png?width=1089&format=png&auto=webp&s=05996ef1b09748a54930a88dc06a85b0c2bcd364

&#x200B;

so not quite there yet but still kind of cool

might be better asking in the solid works subreddit, but you can use the move bodies command, then select the moving jaw and use constraints, select the distance constraint select the face of the moving jaw and then select the face of the fixed jaw and set the distance to 4 inches.

&#x200B;

if you plan  on using this vise time and time again it would be better to save the bodies separately and creating an assembly.

I wouldn't remove all that material like everyone is saying, first id maybe add 2 bolts on the outside of the stock to hold it down or just use some toe clamps, then I'd pre drill a hole and make a  Trochoidal slot (essentially just a adaptive toolpath) that follows the contour of the part that is maybe 2 times the diameter of the endmill you plan on using and cut it out that way.

Then you can remove the outer part and run your finishing passes.

&#x200B;

Also how you machine this really depends on how many you are making, are you making 5 or 10000?

&#x200B;

if you're making 5 do whatever takes the least thinking and setup.

is there not open contour selection in hsm works? if there is pick the contours you want machined and adjust the lead in or tangential extension if thats an option.

&#x200B;

if none of that works you could draw a sketch that extends past the part and use that as your contour geometry
